# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The scene. Matthew 18 opens with a question from the disciples: “At the same time came the disciples unto Jesus, saying, Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?” (18:1). Jesus “called a little child unto him, and set him in the midst of them” (18:2) and spoke of those who are converted and become as little children (18:3). Matthew gives no place for the chapter and no change of place before 18:21. The chapter holds Jesus’ words about “one of these little ones” (18:10) and then a word about a brother who trespasses: go and tell him his fault alone, then take “one or two more,” and then “tell it unto the church” (18:15–17). These verses are reported here as the setting and are not explained.'))
A(('p', '2.2 The question. Matthew then writes, “Then came Peter to him, and said, Lord, how oft shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? till seven times?” (18:21). Jesus answers him in the next verse: “I say not unto thee, Until seven times: but, Until seventy times seven” (18:22). The story begins in the verse after that, and its first word is “Therefore”: “Therefore is the kingdom of heaven likened unto a certain king, which would take account of his servants” (18:23). The text places the story directly after Jesus’ answer to Peter and does not say more about what “Therefore” draws from it.'))
A(('call', 'A Question Comes Before the Story',
   'Peter asks, “how oft shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? till seven times?” (Matthew 18:21). Jesus answers, “Until seventy times seven” (18:22), and then begins the story with “Therefore” (18:23). The text does not say that the story answers Peter’s count, and nothing is added here to make it do so.'))
A(('p', '2.3 The prophetic context. Jesus’ ministry in Matthew is a ministry of the kingdom. “From that time Jesus began to preach, and to say, Repent: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand” (4:17). He sent the Twelve with a limited charge: “Go not into the way of the Gentiles, and into any city of the Samaritans enter ye not: But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel. And as ye go, preach, saying, The kingdom of heaven is at hand” (10:5–7). The disciples in chapter 18 ask about greatness “in the kingdom of heaven,” and the story opens with the same words, “the kingdom of heaven.” The story is read as spoken in that ministry and not as a saying that floats free of it.'))
A(('p', '2.4 Why Jesus tells it here. Matthew gives the occasion: Peter’s question about forgiving a brother and Jesus’ answer to it. The story follows at once. Matthew does not say that Jesus told it for any further reason, and none is supplied here.'))
A(('tbl', 'The verses that set the scene are laid out below.', [
    ['Matt 18:1', '“Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?”', 'The disciples’ question that opens the chapter.'],
    ['Matt 18:2', '“Jesus called a little child unto him”', 'The child set in the midst of them.'],
    ['Matt 18:15', '“if thy brother shall trespass against thee”', 'The word about a brother, before Peter speaks.'],
    ['Matt 18:21', '“how oft shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him?”', 'Peter’s question.'],
    ['Matt 18:22', '“Until seventy times seven”', 'Jesus’ answer to Peter.'],
    ['Matt 18:23', '“Therefore is the kingdom of heaven likened unto a certain king”', 'The story begins.'],
    ['Matt 4:17', '“the kingdom of heaven is at hand”', 'What Jesus preached.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The story is read here in the order Jesus gives it. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another passage, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it.'))
A(('p', '3.1 The opening (18:23). “Therefore is the kingdom of heaven likened unto a certain king, which would take account of his servants.” The comparison is to a certain king who would take account of his servants. The story has a king, servants and an accounting.'))
A(('p', '3.2 The first debt (18:24–25). “And when he had begun to reckon, one was brought unto him, which owed him ten thousand talents. But forasmuch as he had not to pay, his lord commanded him to be sold, and his wife, and children, and all that he had, and payment to be made.” The reckoning begins. One servant is brought, and he owes ten thousand talents. He has “not to pay.” His lord commands that he be sold, with his wife, his children and all that he has, and that payment be made.'))
A(('p', '3.3 The plea and the release (18:26–27). “The servant therefore fell down, and worshipped him, saying, Lord, have patience with me, and I will pay thee all. Then the lord of that servant was moved with compassion, and loosed him, and forgave him the debt.” The servant falls down and asks for patience, and promises to pay all. The lord is “moved with compassion.” He looses him and forgives him the debt.'))
A(('p', '3.4 The fellowservant (18:28–30). “But the same servant went out, and found one of his fellowservants, which owed him an hundred pence: and he laid hands on him, and took him by the throat, saying, Pay me that thou owest. And his fellowservant fell down at his feet, and besought him, saying, Have patience with me, and I will pay thee all. And he would not: but went and cast him into prison, till he should pay the debt.” The servant who was forgiven goes out and finds a fellowservant who owes him a hundred pence. He lays hands on him, takes him by the throat and says, “Pay me that thou owest.” The fellowservant falls down and asks for patience, and promises to pay all. The first servant “would not,” and casts him into prison until he should pay the debt.'))
A(('p', '3.5 The report (18:31). “So when his fellowservants saw what was done, they were very sorry, and came and told unto their lord all that was done.” The other fellowservants see what was done and are very sorry. They go and tell their lord all that was done.'))
A(('p', '3.6 The lord calls him (18:32–33). “Then his lord, after that he had called him, said unto him, O thou wicked servant, I forgave thee all that debt, because thou desiredst me: Shouldest not thou also have had compassion on thy fellowservant, even as I had pity on thee?” The lord calls the servant and speaks to him. He calls him a wicked servant and says that he forgave him all that debt “because thou desiredst me.” He then asks a question: should he not also have had compassion on his fellowservant, even as the lord had pity on him?'))
A(('p', '3.7 The delivery (18:34). “And his lord was wroth, and delivered him to the tormentors, till he should pay all that was due unto him.” The lord is wroth. He delivers the servant to the tormentors until he should pay all that was due. The story ends there.'))
A(('p', '3.8 What follows the story (18:35). Jesus does not end His words at the delivery. The next verse begins, “So likewise shall my heavenly Father do also unto you.” These are Jesus’ own words about the story. They are set out word for word in Section 4, where Jesus’ identifications are recorded, and they are not paraphrased here.'))
A(('p', '3.9 What the story says and does not say. The text says there are two debts, ten thousand talents and an hundred pence, and two pleas, and that the two pleas are made in the same words: “Lord, have patience with me, and I will pay thee all” and “Have patience with me, and I will pay thee all.” It says the first servant is forgiven and the second is cast into prison, and that the lord then calls the first servant, speaks to him and delivers him to the tormentors. It does not say who the king is, who the servants are, what a talent or a penny is worth, or how the first servant came to owe so much. It does not say what the prison or the tormentors are, how long “till he should pay all” lasts, or whether the debt is ever paid. It does not say what happened to the fellowservant after he was cast into prison.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 What Jesus says after the story. After the story Jesus says: “So likewise shall my heavenly Father do also unto you, if ye from your hearts forgive not every one his brother their trespasses” (Matthew 18:35). Everything Jesus identifies in 18:21–35 is in that verse, in the comparison of 18:23 and in His answer to Peter in 18:22. The table records it in His words.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Part of what Jesus says', 'What Jesus says', 'Verse'],
    ['His answer to Peter', '“I say not unto thee, Until seven times: but, Until seventy times seven”', 'Matt 18:22'],
    ['What the kingdom is compared to', '“likened unto a certain king, which would take account of his servants”', 'Matt 18:23'],
    ['How He links the story to His hearers', '“So likewise shall my heavenly Father do also unto you”', 'Matt 18:35'],
    ['Who does it', '“my heavenly Father”', 'Matt 18:35'],
    ['To whom', '“unto you”', 'Matt 18:35'],
    ['On what condition', '“if ye from your hearts forgive not”', 'Matt 18:35'],
    ['Whom they are to forgive', '“every one his brother”', 'Matt 18:35'],
    ['What is to be forgiven', '“their trespasses”', 'Matt 18:35'],
], [0.34, 0.46, 0.20]))
A(('p', '4.2 What Jesus does not name in the story. Jesus names a certain king, a heavenly Father who does “so likewise,” hearers who are addressed as “you,” and a brother whose trespasses are forgiven or not. He leaves the parts of the story without an identification. Jesus does not identify the king, who is also called the lord of the servant. Jesus does not identify the servant who owed ten thousand talents. Jesus does not identify the ten thousand talents. Jesus does not identify the wife and children who were to be sold with him. Jesus does not identify the fellowservant who owed an hundred pence, or the hundred pence. Jesus does not identify the prison. Jesus does not identify the fellowservants who saw what was done and told their lord. Jesus does not identify the tormentors, or what “all that was due unto him” is. Jesus does not state in so many words that the king of the story is the heavenly Father of 18:35. He says “So likewise shall my heavenly Father do also unto you,” and does not say more about how the king and the Father stand to each other. Jesus does not state that the first servant stands for the hearers of 18:35, or that the fellowservant is the brother of 18:35.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus names only a certain king, the heavenly Father who does so likewise, the hearers addressed as “you,” and a brother whose trespasses are forgiven or not, nothing further is named here. A reader who says that the king is a certain person, that the servant is a certain people, that the debt is a certain thing, that the prison or the tormentors are a certain place or power, or that the fellowservants are certain persons is supplying what the Lord did not.'))
A(('p', '4.3 The explanation stays with the story. The words of 18:35 belong to this story and are not carried to another. The same word “debt,” the same word “forgive” and the same word “trespasses” stand in other sayings of Jesus, and those sayings are not used here to supply what 18:35 leaves unsaid.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'This section states the meaning that arises from the comparison itself. It repeats none of Jesus’ identifications from Section 4 and assigns an identity to no part of the story.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison. In Matthew the comparison is of the kingdom of heaven to a certain king who takes account of his servants. The meaning of the comparison is in the movement of the story: a debt too large to pay, a release, a refusal of a far smaller debt, a report, a call and a judgment.'))
A(('pk', '5.2 What the comparison communicates about the kingdom of heaven.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**An account is taken.** The king “would take account of his servants.” The comparison puts a reckoning inside the kingdom of heaven.',
    '**One debt is released.** A servant who has “not to pay” is loosed and forgiven the whole debt, in a story that opens on his own plea and the lord’s compassion.',
    '**A refusal follows a release.** The servant who was forgiven finds a fellowservant who owes a far smaller sum, takes him by the throat and casts him into prison. The same plea, “have patience with me,” does not move him.',
    '**The refusal is seen and reported.** The other fellowservants are “very sorry” and tell their lord all that was done. The story does not leave the refusal unnoticed.',
    '**The reversal is stated by the lord.** He calls the servant wicked, reminds him that he forgave “all that debt,” and asks whether he should not have had compassion, “even as I had pity on thee.”',
    '**The judgment is given.** The lord is wroth and delivers him to the tormentors “till he should pay all that was due unto him.”',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 The weight of the comparison. The story sets two sums side by side, ten thousand talents and an hundred pence, and two pleas in the same words, and two outcomes, a release and a prison. The comparison communicates that mercy received is set beside mercy refused, and that the refusal is called wicked by the one who gave the release. The story does not say what the two sums equal. The weight is in the contrast of what was forgiven and what was refused.'))
A(('p', '5.4 What the hearers were meant to understand. The hearers were Peter and the disciples who had asked about greatness in the kingdom of heaven and about forgiving a brother (18:1, 21). Jesus says “unto you” in 18:35. They were meant to understand, from the story, that in the kingdom of heaven as Jesus compares it, a servant who has been released from a great debt and will not release another from a small one is judged. The text says this is a comparison about the kingdom of heaven in Jesus’ earthly ministry. It is not a comparison about the church of the Body.'))
A(('p', '5.5 What this section does not do. It does not say who the king is, or what the servant, the debts, the prison or the tormentors are. It does not say who the fellowservants are. It does not turn forgiving, refusing, reporting or paying into steps by which a person is saved or lost. It draws no meaning from another parable into this story.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The Unforgiving Servant belongs to Matthew 18. It is kept apart here from three neighbors.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 18:12–14', '“How think ye? if a man have an hundred sheep”', 'The Lost Sheep and the Lost Coin. The sheep stands earlier in the same chapter, and the sheep and the coin also stand in Luke 15. It is a story of a loss and a search, and has its own study. The debt is not read into it and it is not read into the debt.'],
    ['Luke 7:41–42', '“a certain creditor which had two debtors”', 'The Two Debtors. Told in a Pharisee’s house about a creditor and two debtors who could not pay, and followed by Jesus’ question about love. It has its own study. It is not a story of a refusal or a delivery.'],
    ['Matt 20:1', '“the kingdom of heaven is like unto a man that is an householder”', 'The Laborers in the Vineyard. Another comparison of the kingdom of heaven in Matthew, about hired laborers and a reckoning of wages in chapter 20. It has its own study. It is not a story of debt or forgiveness.'],
], [0.17, 0.33, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The Unforgiving Servant is told after Peter’s question about forgiving a brother (Matthew 18:21–22). The Lost Sheep in Matthew stands after the question about greatness and the child (18:1–14). The Two Debtors is told in a Pharisee’s house (Luke 7:36–40). The Laborers in the Vineyard stands in Matthew 20, after a question from Peter in chapter 19. Each is read in its own place.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The Unforgiving Servant has a king, a servant who owes ten thousand talents, a fellowservant who owes an hundred pence, other fellowservants and tormentors. The sheep has a man and a sheep, the Two Debtors a creditor and two debtors, and the Laborers a householder and hired laborers. No character is carried from one account to another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The Unforgiving Servant moves from a reckoning to a release, a refusal, a report and a delivery. The Lost Sheep moves from a straying to a seeking and a rejoicing. The Two Debtors moves from a debt that two could not pay to a question about love. The Laborers moves from a hiring to a reckoning of wages. None of those movements is read into this story.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, “seventy times seven” in Matthew 18:22 is Jesus’ answer to Peter, and the “seven times in a day” of Luke 17:4 is a saying in another place; neither is used to explain the other. Second, the “debts” and “trespasses” of Matthew 6:12, 14–15 are words in another saying, and they are not used to explain the debt of 18:27 or the trespasses of 18:35. Third, the “tormentors” of 18:34 are not matched with any other place of judgment, and the “prison” of 18:30 is not matched with any prison in another story.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks to Peter and to the disciples who asked about greatness in the kingdom of heaven (Matthew 18:1, 21). The story belongs to the earthly ministry in which He preached that the kingdom of heaven is at hand (4:17) and sent the Twelve to the lost sheep of the house of Israel (10:5–7).'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The Unforgiving Servant was told before any of that, and it is not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Nothing in Matthew 18:21–35 speaks of the Body of Christ, and the passage is not read as though the Body were already formed. The Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul is read in Paul’s letters.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The king, the servant, the debts, the release, the refusal and the delivery are not a description of the Body of Christ. The story is not read as a universal picture of how any person is forgiven by God. The condition Jesus states in Matthew 18:35 is spoken in His earthly ministry, and it is not carried over as a condition for the Body. Forgiving, refusing and paying are actions in a story and a saying, and they are not a mechanism of salvation. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this story: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9). Paul writes to the Body about forgiving one another in his own words: “And be ye kind one to another, tenderhearted, forgiving one another, even as God for Christ’s sake hath forgiven you” (Ephesians 4:32). That sentence is read in Paul’s letter and is not joined to the condition of Matthew 18:35.'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the comparison shows. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of mercy received.** The servant asks for patience and is released. A reader learns to weigh what he has been forgiven when another asks the same of him.',
    '**A lesson of the same plea.** The fellowservant makes the same plea in the same words and is refused. A reader learns to hear in another’s plea what he has pleaded himself.',
    '**A lesson of a refusal that is seen.** The other fellowservants see what was done and tell their lord. A reader learns that a refusal of mercy is not hidden.',
    '**A lesson of forgiving from the heart.** Jesus says “from your hearts.” A reader learns that the forgiveness He names is not a form of words only.',
    '**A lesson of weighing a debt.** The story sets ten thousand talents beside an hundred pence. A reader learns to weigh what another owes against what has been forgiven him.',
]))
A(('p', 'None of these lessons says that the Body is the servant, that the Body is the fellowservant, or that the Body is under the condition of Matthew 18:35. They are principles drawn from what the story shows.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'Peter asks how often his brother may sin against him and be forgiven, and Jesus answers, “Until seventy times seven.” Jesus then compares the kingdom of heaven to a certain king who takes account of his servants. One servant owes ten thousand talents, has not to pay, and is ordered sold with his wife and children. He falls down and asks for patience, and the lord is moved with compassion, looses him and forgives him the debt. That servant then finds a fellowservant who owes an hundred pence, takes him by the throat and casts him into prison when he asks for the same patience. The other fellowservants report it. The lord calls the servant wicked, reminds him that he forgave him all that debt, and delivers him to the tormentors till he should pay all that was due. Jesus then says, “So likewise shall my heavenly Father do also unto you, if ye from your hearts forgive not every one his brother their trespasses.” Jesus names nothing else in the story. He spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says who the king, the servant or the fellowservant is, or that makes forgiving a way of being saved, says more than the text says.'))
A(('pk', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a question from Peter and an answer, then a reckoning, a debt, a release, a refusal of a smaller debt, a report, a call and a delivery to the tormentors.',
    '**The meaning:** mercy received is set beside mercy refused, and the refusal is called wicked and judged.',
    '**The boundaries:** only a certain king, the heavenly Father who does so likewise, the hearers addressed as “you,” and a brother are named; the servant, the fellowservant, the debts, the prison and the tormentors are not.',
    '**The present lesson:** mercy received, the same plea heard, a refusal seen, forgiving from the heart and a debt weighed, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

